Figure 3 shows the local merger rate density of BH-NS systems as a function of \\sigma (see Eq. 3) and for different assumed values of \\alpha. We also show the BH-NS merger rate estimated empirically by LVK. We see that for \\sigma\\leq 150 km s{}^{-1}, BH-NS merger rates are about 50 Gpc{}^{-3} yr{}^{-1} and 150 Gpc{}^{-3} yr{}^{-1} for \\alpha=1 and \\alpha=3, respectively, about twice what we get for \\sigma=260 km s{}^{-1}. When \\alpha=5, we estimate that the merger rates are about 400 Gpc{}^{-3} yr{}^{-1}, 300 Gpc{}^{-3} yr{}^{-1}, 200 Gpc{}^{-3} yr{}^{-1}, 100 Gpc{}^{-3} yr{}^{-1} for \\sigma=10 km s{}^{-1}, \\sigma=40 km s{}^{-1}, \\sigma=150 km s{}^{-1}, \\sigma=260 km s{}^{-1}, respectively. While our inferred merger rates are in agreement with the LVK collaboration's lower estimate, they can account for their higher estimate only if \\alpha=3 or \\alpha=5, regardless of the assumed value for \\sigma. When assuming \\sigma\\leq 40 km s{}^{-1} and \\alpha=5, our predicted merger rates become too high.

